atrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c91d6d-d971-5458-b5b6-a791fcbf9443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241266} {:answer/id #uuid "42963ae3-0b38-512b-8bfd-1ad2c0d6d6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241779} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd12c13-09d7-5e3b-908d-be282fb53d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242558} {:answer/id #uuid "c9987249-694e-5f70-95b6-77fd36db1778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242956} {:answer/id #uuid "fc018d5e-5612-54cf-81be-1c70e01d83d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc018d5e-5612-54cf-81be-1c70e01d83d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type