}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0857eda5-eeea-55ef-98f6-53654c385d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241250} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec430f7-7c2f-5509-bf32-e90bd49e5ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241448} {:answer/id #uuid "854a3cea-9af5-57ca-889a-18380bc93538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242345} {:answer/id #uuid "c660c215-abc4-52f5-b033-211ea838c77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\