cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0103ed51-38eb-56e1-b5b6-506ac5114dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241194} {:answer/id #uuid "28f58628-e349-51a5-9381-2a6c3f3e63d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241538} {:answer/id #uuid "362edb3b-c2c6-5e35-b27f-a4726544a314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241655} {:answer/id #uuid "88c00484-3dac-50cd-9b40-2f30d2fe5429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242384} {:answer/id #uuid "d8290906-4330-5010-8b90-d9121d42fcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8290906-4330-5010-8b90-d9121d42fcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243101} :answer-field/id #uuid "97841af7-3445-571b-82d4-01c790eeec83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "715f2529-cb8e-5152-b727-a6588c23a27e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106856" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{3}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-2,$ ${u}_{33}=3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f8bad-4488-5247-b030-e69c65d69aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241189} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0029bc-5dd1-5f3b-a321-07