5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0dffe5-8c18-5c35-922b-ab2349cf0224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$826$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270230} {:answer/id #uuid "775e536f-38c2-5ab0-af53-9c42718f6442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$868$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270326} {:answer/id #uuid "93d8e9e4-fad7-52e1-8058-4325cb48029f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270562} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89e73c-4770-571b-b13d-e6698523a25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$902$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270927} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3d4783-4f4a-52b1-a174-c83258ea7302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$872$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d8e9e4-fad7-52e1-8058-4325cb48029f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270562} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4a8479-bce9-519b-bc40-a906989c59fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51f93e5f-4b65-5909-b2fb-547fe7d03d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-139736" :question/problem "A statistician collected data on the height above the ground (in hundreds of meters) of a hot air balloon during the first five minutes of its flight. This data is shown below. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.6$ | $3.0$ | $4.6$ | $6$ | $7.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the balloon's height after $6$ minutes, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304fe9af-3557-5028-a8cb-089a0423c966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 29 \\\\ 4 & 22 \\\\ 5 & 41\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269704} {:answer/id #uuid "977d7ff5-a3ee-5e2b-b0b3-c9d82373321e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 29 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 41\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270600} {:answer/id #uuid "bddaef05-98e6-515f-95ec-862c77f52ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 29 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 41 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270943} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df969f-7857-5ac8-a767-ab6610c2e53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271156} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6be42a-2f5b-5e96-8b4d-d9cf1fd82c23" :answer/type {: